To move from collecting the record to reading it, we need the following:
1. The signed inventor assignments, or the employment agreements relied on in=
 their place, for any family where the recorded chain does not run from every=
 named inventor to the licensor. We can request these from the licensor's cou=
nsel directly if you would rather not relay them.
2. An editable copy of the patent schedule attached to the draft license, so =
that we can mark it up family by family.
3. A product description or data sheet for what Price-Henson intends to make =
or sell under the license, since that decides which claims we read against th=
e product first.

Where the record shows a gap or an encumbrance, I will set out what the docum=
ents say and leave the question of what it means for the license to counsel, =
who will give you that view in writing once the record is complete.

